<article class="env-lemma" id="0BXZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BXZ">Lemma <span data-tag="0BXZ">53.2.2</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a normal curve and $Y$ a proper variety. The set of rational maps from $X$ to $Y$ is the same as the set of morphisms $X \to Y$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      A rational map from $X$ to $Y$ can be extended to a morphism $X \to Y$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0BXY" data-tag="0BXY">53.2.1</a> as every local ring is a discrete valuation ring (for example by Varieties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B8Y" data-tag="0B8Y">33.43.8</a>). Conversely, if two morphisms $f,g: X \to Y$ are equivalent as rational maps, then $f = g$ by Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01RH" data-tag="01RH">29.7.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>